course: dictionary
lesson: 1661
title: Chivalry and the knights' code
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Chivalry is a noun. It names a set of rules and ideals, not a thing you can touch. The sentence gives you the clues: «Chivalry was the code knights were meant to follow: be brave and loyal, protect the weak and honour God.» A code is a set of rules, so chivalry is the rules that knights tried to live by.
explain: The sentence also says «real knights often fell short of it». Fell short means did not reach the standard. So the ideal was high, and people did not always manage it. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? A word like rules or ideals fits, so chivalry is the ideal that knights aimed for.
explain: Today the word is used for everyday kindness too. When someone is polite, kind and generous, especially holding a door for others, we might say they show chivalry. Read the words around the word, swap in your meaning, and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Chivalry is an uncountable noun, so we say chivalry, not a chivalry.
fact: «Chivalry» means the rules and ideals medieval knights were supposed to follow: being brave, loyal, honest and protecting the weak.
fact: Knights also served God and their lord, so chivalry included loyalty and faith.
fact: Today «chivalry» can mean polite, kind and generous behaviour, such as holding a door open for someone.
fact: «Fell short» means did not reach the standard; real knights often fell short of the ideal.
exercise: «Chivalry was the code knights were meant to follow.» What is chivalry? | A type of castle built on a hill | A long journey across the sea | A kind of armour worn by horses | *The rules of brave, loyal behaviour that knights tried to follow
exercise: The sentence says real knights «fell short of it». What does fell short mean? | Climbed a very steep hill | Were very tall | Fell over in a race | *Did not reach the standard
exercise: Which everyday action shows chivalry? | *Holding a door open for someone | Ignoring a guest at the door | Taking a seat without asking | Shouting across the playground
test: In the sentence, what were knights meant to do for the weak? | Laugh at them | Ignore them | *Protect them | Make them work for them
test: What does chivalry mean in everyday life? | Being rude to strangers | Riding a horse very fast | Fighting every day | *Polite, kind and generous behaviour
exam: Why is chivalry linked with stories of King Arthur? | *Because the knights in those stories were meant to follow the code of chivalry | Because the stories are all about cooking | Because chivalry is a type of food | Because Arthur invented the code of chivalry in a single day
